American-born children, who, even though there was no evidence that Japanese or their children were conspiring against the US government or against American society. The government decided to err on the side of caution and round it up this population and put them in internment camps. It was one of the most shameful periods in American history. Those who were interned and their children felt the consequences of that executive decision for many, many decades afterwards. And so that's just one example.
在美国出生的孩子，尽管没有证据表明日本人或他们的孩子在密谋反对美国政府或美国社会。政府决定采取谨慎态度，将这群人围捕起来并送进拘留营。这是美国历史上最耻辱的时期之一。那些被关押的人及其后代在之后的几十年里一直感受到那个行政决定的后果。这只是一个例子。

27
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:56.000
But also during World War II, there were many opportunities that we lost to accommodate people fleeing Europe. And we lost that opportunity because we feared, again, we were concerned with national security. We feared that among the refugees, among the immigrants, would be spies and saboteurs that would undermine the United States. But those fears prevented us from accommodating many, many people who were fleeing the third Reich and needed protection, and we lost that opportunity to help them. When you look at the experience of Jewish immigrants and refugees around the time of World War II, around the 1930s, 1940s,
但在二战期间，我们也失去了许多接纳逃离欧洲的人的机会。我们之所以失去这个机会，是因为我们害怕，再一次，我们担心国家安全。我们担心在难民和移民中会有会破坏美国的间谍和破坏分子。但这些恐惧使我们无法接纳许多许多逃离第三帝国并需要保护的人，我们失去了帮助他们的机会。当你回顾二战前后（大约20世纪30年代、40年代）犹太移民和难民的经历时，

28
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:27.000
paint me a picture of what happened. The popular narrative is that there are many European Jews who did make it to the United States, many of whom were involved in the scientific research that had helped the United States eventually win World War II. But was that sort of a general experience? Did the United States was the United States generally welcoming to Jewish refugees from Europe in the middle of the 20th century? No, we weren't actually. Many historians feel that our policies were highly
请给我描绘一下当时的情况。普遍的说法是，有许多欧洲犹太人确实设法来到了美国，其中许多人参与了帮助美国最终赢得二战的科学研究。但这是一种普遍的经历吗？在20世纪中叶，美国普遍欢迎来自欧洲的犹太难民吗？不，事实上我们没有。许多历史学家认为我们的政策非常

29
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:57.000
anti-Semitic during this period. We lost many opportunities to allow Jewish refugees to come to the United States. The most classic example is the SSA in Louis. Ship carrying over 900 passengers who were fleeing Europe. And when they arrived off the American coast, the people on the ship were prevented from coming to the United States. The ship was turned away. And eventually, you know, the ship traveled to many different countries.
在这段时期内排犹。我们错失了许多允许犹太难民来到美国的机会。最典型的例子就是圣路易斯号。这艘船载着900多名逃离欧洲的乘客。当他们到达美国海岸附近时，船上的人被阻止进入美国。这艘船被赶走了。最终，你知道的，这艘船去了许多不同的国家。

30
00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:30.000
They were also denied the opportunity to land. And eventually the ship was forced to return to Europe. And many of the people who were traveling on the SSC, Lewis eventually were caught and sent to camps, to the death camps. So when we look at the broad sweep of history here, we sort of see this pattern repeatedly occurring where groups arrive and within a few decades or a couple of generations of being here, they have concerns about newcomers entering their neighborhoods.
他们登陆的机会也被剥夺了。最终，这艘船被迫返回欧洲。许多乘坐圣路易斯号旅行的人最终被捕并被送往集中营、死亡集中营。因此，当我们纵观这段宏大的历史时，我们会发现这种模式反复出现：群体抵达后，在来到这里的短短几十年或几代人内，他们又开始对进入其社区的新来者产生担忧。

31
00:16:30.000 --> 00:17:03.000
And in some ways, we seem to have a positive view of immigration and immigrants when they were long ago and far away. But less so when they're here now, when the family that arrived last year that lives next door. And I'm wondering if you have insight into why this happens? Why is it that groups that themselves may have faced challenges as immigrants? How is it that within a couple of decades or maybe a couple of generations, they are turning around and having the same concerns that were expressed about them not so long ago?
在某种程度上，当移民在很久以前、遥远的地方时，我们似乎对移民和移民抱有积极的看法。但当他们就在这里、现在，当去年到达并住在隔壁的家庭出现时，这种看法就不那么积极了。我想知道你是否对为什么会发生这种事有什么见解？为什么那些自己作为移民时可能面临挑战的群体，在短短几十年或几代人之内，就会回过头来产生不久前别人对他们表达过的同样的担忧？

32
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:51.000
Well that seems to be the pattern. I guess you could say that part of Americanization is to adopt the values and the perspectives of the society that surrounds you. And it also becomes a defense mechanism. It becomes a way of proving your membership in the society to adopt those values and to reflect those values out, to demonstrate that you are a member of the in-group and not the out-group. You recently wrote an essay about the current debates over immigration, in which you said, generations from now, students in U.S. history classes, many of them, the children of immigrants arriving today, will read what our political candidates, editorialists, bloggers, and talking heads had to say about their ancestors, and shudder. What did you mean by that?
嗯，这似乎就是规律。我想你可以说，美国化的一部分就是采纳包围你的社会的价值观和视角。这也成了一种防御机制。采纳这些价值观并将其反射出去，以此来证明你是内群体而不是外群体的一员，这成了证明你在社会中成员身份的一种方式。你最近写了一篇关于当前移民辩论的文章，其中提到，几代人之后，美国历史课堂上的学生——其中许多人是今天到达的移民的儿童——会读到我们的政治候选人、社论作者、博主和名嘴对他们的祖先所说的话，然后感到不寒而栗。你这话是什么意思？

33
00:17:51.000 --> 00:19:05.000
When I teach courses in immigration history and we read the editorials and the newspaper articles and the newspaper articles and the broadsides that were published in the 19th and in the early 20th century. My students often shake their heads and wonder how Americans could have ever had those feelings or their thoughts or perspectives, they laugh nervously. But then when we compare those editorials and newspaper articles and broadsides to some of the editorials and blogs and newspaper articles today, they see the continuities. And so I suspect that 20 years from now, 30 years from now, I suspect that my students reading the thoughts, the articles of today, will also find them ridiculous, will also shudder, will also wonder why Americans felt the way they did.
当我教移民史课程时，我们阅读19世纪和20世纪初出版的社论、报纸文章和传单。我的学生经常摇头，想知道美国人怎么会有那种感觉、想法或视角，他们会紧张地笑。但随后我们将这些社论、报纸文章和传单与当今的一些社论、博客和报纸文章进行比较时，他们看到了连贯性。因此我猜想，20年、30年后，我的学生在阅读今天的思想和文章时，也会觉得它们荒谬可笑，也会不寒而栗，也会纳闷为什么美国人会有那样的感受。

34
00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:34.000
But I hope not. There's this part of me that hopes that we will do better, that we will be better. That was historian Maria Christina Garcia. She's a professor of American studies at Cornell University. Hidden Brain is produced by Jenny Schmidt, Maggie Penman, Raina Cohen, René Clair and Path Shah. Our supervising producer is Tara Boyle.
